# Package a desktop app

Rocci desktop apps are local web applications: an authored or generated Roc
server listens on loopback, while Tao and Wry display that origin in a native
window. The UI remains usable in a normal browser.

## Configure the app

Create `rocci.toml` beside the project:

```toml
[app]
name = "My Tool"
identifier = "dev.example.my-tool"
version = "0.1.0"

[[windows]]
label = "main"
title = "My Tool"
url = "/"
width = 1040
height = 760
min_width = 720
min_height = 560

[http]
host = "127.0.0.1"
port = 0

[assets]
directory = "assets"
embed = true
datastar = "1.0.2"

[bundle]
app = "."
```

`port = 0` chooses a free local port. The runtime accepts only loopback hosts.

## Validate before bundling

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- validate rocci.toml
```

Unknown keys are errors. Validation also checks app identifiers, unique window
labels, URLs, security settings, and bundle resource paths.

## Build the bundle

On macOS:

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- bundle --config rocci.toml
```

The command compiles the Roc app, builds the native host, assembles an `.app`,
and applies an ad-hoc signature. The bundled app does not need `roc` on the
runtime `PATH`.

## Linux server binary (opt-in OCI)

`rocci build --release` writes a Roc `server` plus `assets/`, not a `.app`.
Reuse that tree in a slim process image with `uv run --project tools/rocci-ops rocci-ops serve app`. Compose
sets `ROC_BASIC_WEBSERVER_HOST=0.0.0.0` so the container is reachable; do not
change `[http].host` in `rocci.toml` (loopback validation stays). Linux OCI is
opt-in. Windows installers are not implemented.

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-cli -- build --release examples/rocci/custom/datastar --target arm64musl
uv run --project tools/rocci-ops rocci-ops serve app target/release/rocci-server
```

Match the container CPU (`arm64musl` on Apple Silicon Docker; `x64musl` on
amd64). See `docker/README.md`.

## Understand the current boundary

- Desktop packaging is macOS-only (ad-hoc `.app`).
- Linux server packaging is opt-in (`rocci build --release` plus optional OCI).
- The signature is suitable for local development, not distribution.
- Production signing, notarization, installers, and updates are not implemented.
- The host currently manages one application window.
- Native capability APIs beyond the webview are planned, not available.

Use the [configuration reference](/reference/configuration/) for every field
before planning a release workflow.
